Now I see the full picture:

```typescript
// Line 185
export function resolveConfigPath(
  env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv = {},
  stateDir: string = resolveStateDir(env, envHomedir(env))
): string {
  const override = env.OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH?.trim();
  if (override) return override;
  return path.join(stateDir, "openclaw.json");
}
```

So:
- `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH` env var takes priority
- If not set, falls back to `$STATE_DIR/openclaw.json`

And `STATE_DIR`:
```typescript
export function resolveStateDir(env = {}, envHomedir = homedir) {
  const override = env.OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR?.trim();
  if (override) return override;
  // For root user, STATE_DIR = /root/.openclaw
  // NOT /opt/cclaw-client/.../data!
  return path.join(homedir(), NEW_STATE_DIRNAME); // /root/.openclaw
}
```

This is the root cause! When running as root without the env vars, it reads from `/root/.openclaw/openclaw.json`. When I edited the config file at `/opt/cclaw-client/.../data/openclaw.json`, the systemd gateway process was actually reading from `/root/.openclaw/openclaw.json`, which already had a token configured but was a different token value than what I set in the data directory.

